1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,500
You are seventeen, and the first thing the navy hands you
is not a sword. It is an oar.

2
00:00:06,500 --> 00:00:13,000
Three hundred men pull together below deck, and none of them can see where the ship is going.

3
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:20,000
Before this video ends, you will stand on the stern and give the order to ram.

4
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:28,500
Every rank on a Roman warship had its own pay, its own food and its own way to die.

5
00:00:28,500 --> 00:00:40,000
We will climb all of them, one bench at a time, from the bottom of the hull to the captain's chair.

6
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:52,000
The rower sits on a narrow bench with a leather cushion, and he keeps the stroke by the beat of the piper.

7
00:00:52,000 --> 00:01:05,000
He is a free man, not a slave, and he is paid in silver, about as much as a farm labourer on land.

8
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:20,000
Miss the rhythm, and the oar behind you breaks your back. Rowers learned the beat before they learned the sea.

9
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:30,000
But one rower in every crew was watching the deck above, waiting for a chance to climb.

10
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:45,000
The marine stands on that deck in armour, ready to board the enemy once the beaks lock together.

11
00:01:45,000 --> 00:02:10,000
He earns more than the rower, eats meat twice a week, and is the first man the archers aim at.

12
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:25,000
The helmsman holds two steering oars at the stern and reads the water like a farmer reads the sky.

13
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:45,000
Above them all is the trierarch, the captain, who answers for the ship with his own money and his name.

14
00:02:45,000 --> 00:03:00,000
So when the order to ram comes, it travels down three hundred backs at once. Now you know whose voice it is.
